<StoryLayout story={story}>
  <div class="container">
<section class="section" id="intro">
<h2><span class="num">01 — Context</span>The Rule and the Disclosure</h2>

<p>On the first page of a defense brief filed October 29, 2026, in the Northern District of Texas, a disclosure appeared under the heading "Use of Generative Artificial Intelligence": "Portions of this brief were prepared with the assistance of a generative artificial intelligence tool. Local Criminal Rule 47.2(e)." Four days later, federal prosecutors answered with a footnote full of percentages.</p>

<p>The rule that produced the disclosure is not new: the court's online criminal rules, headed "Local Rules Effective September 3, 2024," already contain it. N.D. Tex. Local Criminal Rule 47.2(e) requires any brief prepared using generative AI to state this on its first page under a standard heading. Omission of the disclosure certifies that no AI was used. The rule does not prohibit AI assistance. It permits it, on the condition of disclosure.</p>

<p>The rule also provides a mechanism for going further. Subsection (e)(1) states: "If the presiding judge so directs, the party filing the brief must disclose the specific parts prepared using generative artificial intelligence." The architecture is two-tiered. A mandatory first-page flag as the default. A judicial directive available if the court wants more. The rule's own route to learning which parts were AI-prepared runs through the presiding judge.</p>

<p>The brief was filed in United States v. Bhengura. Khalwinder Singh Bhengura, a 70-year-old lawful permanent resident, had been convicted on October 27, 2026, of making a false statement of citizenship — a felony — and of voting by an alien — a misdemeanor. The jury acquitted him on a second false-statement count. He was represented by three retained counsel: Mario Herrera, Nicole E. Knox, and Vincent Rene Carrizales II.</p>

<p>The relationship between defense counsel and the court had strained before any AI issue arose. On September 24, Judge Mark T. Pittman ordered Knox to show cause why she should not be sanctioned, citing "caustic filings towards the Court." On October 19, Knox was hospitalized. Herrera moved for a continuance; the government agreed; the court denied the motion. According to the defense's own chronology, trial began October 23 without her. An order entered October 27, the day of the verdict, records that after "numerous and continuous duplicative, frivolous, and obstructive objections," Herrera was warned that further disruptive interjections would result in sanction.</p>

<p>Two days after the verdict, defense counsel filed ECF 77: a brief in support of an emergency motion for Bhengura's immediate release pending sentencing. The first page carried the AI disclosure required by LCrR 47.2(e). The rule had been followed.</p>

