<section class="section" id="footnote">
<h2><span class="num">02 — What Happened</span>The Footnote</h2>

<p>On November 2, 2026, Assistant United States Attorney Matthew Capoccia filed the government's opposition to Bhengura's emergency motion (ECF 81). Footnote 1, spanning pages 4 and 5 of the twenty-page filing, disclosed that the government had run four defense filings through "the AI-spotting tool from Pangram" and reported the results.</p>

<p>The percentages: Docket No. 57 scored 76% AI-generated. Docket No. 75 scored 63%. Docket No. 77 — the brief that carried the first-page AI disclosure — scored 63% excluding its factual chronology, and 46% including it. Docket No. 80 scored 63%. The footnote points to Exhibits A through D for the results. Of the four defense filings scanned, only ECF 77 is available on the public record. Whether Docket Nos. 57, 75, and 80 also carried the LCrR 47.2(e) AI disclosure is unknown.</p>

<p>The government also ran four of its own briefs through the same tool. Docket Nos. 25, 28, 56, and 60 all scored 0% AI-generated. The government stated this was done "to see if the tool was overly aggressive in spotting AI-generated text." These results were attached as Exhibits E through H.</p>

<p>The government's characterization of the defense filings was direct. "Defendant appears to be almost entirely using AI to generate them," the footnote stated. "Virtually nothing of substance in these filings seems to have been drafted by a human on the Defense team." The government also alleged that discovery emails sent by the defense in the week before trial were "100% AI drafted."</p>

<p>The government acknowledged that "the local rules do permit the use of generative AI under some conditions." Its argument was that "what has occurred in this case is beyond what the United States believes the rules contemplate." The filing characterized the case as "defense by ChatGPT" and the briefs as "AI slop," writing that "motions practice in this case is the equivalent of writing responses to documents generated by a robot that is attempting to practice law."</p>

