<section class="section" id="mechanism">
<h2><span class="num">03 — The Mechanism</span>The Detector and the Rule</h2>

<p>Pangram's own guidance page, last reviewed October 9, 2026, explains what its percentages represent. "The percentage is a share of the text, not a confidence level," the vendor states. "A 99% AI result means Pangram labeled 99% of the text, by character, as AI-generated. It does not mean Pangram is 99% sure."</p>

<p>The same guidance warns against acting on a detection result alone. Pangram states that "any positive detection could be real, or it could be the statistically anomalous one-in-ten-thousand situation where Pangram gets it wrong." The vendor recommends checking per-segment confidence levels before drawing conclusions: "A high percentage made up of low-confidence segments is weaker evidence than the same percentage with high-confidence segments, so check both before you act." In Pangram's own words, detection warrants investigation "before any punitive measures."</p>

<p>Pangram's documentation describes the boundary between human and AI writing as inherently uncertain. "Co-authorship is a spectrum," the vendor states, "and defining the exact line between these categories is more of an art than a science."</p>

<p>According to Pangram's self-published technical report (first posted February 2024), the tool was benchmarked on ten text domains: blog posts, scientific writing, news, books, student writing, email, creative writing, reviews, Q&amp;A, and Wikipedia. Legal briefs were not among them. The benchmark tested eight large language models; Claude was excluded. The report states this was due to "several observed issues getting Claude to respond correctly to our prompts." Pangram claims 99% accuracy on 1,976 benchmark documents — a vendor-reported figure from a self-published paper that has not been independently peer-reviewed. The 2024 report may not reflect the version of Pangram the government used in late 2026.</p>

<p>LCrR 47.2(e)(1) provides a pathway for the same question the detector purports to answer: how much of this brief is AI-generated? The rule's pathway runs through the presiding judge, who can direct the filing party to identify the specific parts prepared with AI. The public docket in United States v. Bhengura does not show Judge Pittman invoking this provision. Instead, a party deployed a commercial tool and filed the resulting number. The percentage looks far more specific than a first-page disclosure — but it arrived through a pathway with no judicial directive, no adversarial testing of the tool's methodology, and no domain validation for legal briefs.</p>

<p>The government's approach has its own logic. The percentages convey something that bare disclosure — "Portions of this brief" — does not: an estimate of extent. The government ran its own filings as a control and all scored 0%. It acknowledged the rule permits AI "under some conditions." Its argument was not that AI use was categorically prohibited but that the defense had exceeded what the rules contemplate. These are legitimate advocacy choices by a party in an adversarial proceeding.</p>

<p>Separately, a broader class of reliability questions attends AI text detection. A 2023 study by Liang et al. found that commercial GPT detectors consistently misclassified non-native English writing as AI-generated — a finding that Pangram's own technical report cites and addresses with a TOEFL benchmark showing 0% false positives on 91 non-native essays, according to the vendor. This finding does not apply to the specific facts of this case. The defense briefs were authored by counsel — Herrera, Knox, and Carrizales — not by the non-native-speaking defendant. But the study illustrates the class of validation questions that remain open for AI text detectors as a category.</p>

