</section>

<section class="section" id="ruling">
<h2><span class="num">04 — Consequence</span>The Ruling</h2>

<p>On November 4, two days after the government's opposition, Judge Pittman issued his Memorandum Opinion and Order (ECF 83). The analysis opened with a characterization of the defense filings: "Defendant's arguments for immediate release are mostly generated by artificial intelligence and fail several times over. Unfortunately, this has become a habit for defense counsel."</p>

<p>The AI criticism ran through the opinion. Judge Pittman called the defense's statutory argument "a tortured statutory analysis so tone-deaf that only artificial intelligence could advance it." He called the defense's citation of United States v. Mincey "at best misleading, but more likely dishonest," explaining that Mincey involved supervised-release violations and "had nothing to do with a conviction for which the Guidelines recommend zero to six months." Mincey is a real case. The judge's criticism was misapplication — using a case about one legal context to argue about a materially different one. He called the citation "a prime example of the inherent dangers of lawyers relying on artificial intelligence to draft their briefs."</p>

<p>In the opinion's conclusion, Judge Pittman wrote in a single sentence: "This Court is disturbed by counsel's repeated filings which subtly bend the law by proffering superficially persuasive but fundamentally meritless arguments so clearly generated by artificial intelligence."</p>

<p>Footnote 2, on page 6, contained the reference to the government's detector filing. The judge cited "ECF No. 81 at 4" — the page of ECF 81 that carries the Pangram footnote with its percentages. He wrote that the court "is sympathetic to the pleas of the United States," which, in the footnote's words, "has had to contend with 'defense by ChatGPT.'" He added that the court "would sanction counsel but, due to its demanding docket, has no time to schedule a hearing between now and Thanksgiving." He wrote that the Local Rules "do not permit such blatant 'AI slop'" and warned that "further waste of the Court and Government's limited resources will result in the imposition of sanctions, up to and including banning counsel's ability to practice law in this district." The judge did not mention Pangram by name. He did not cite any specific percentage.</p>

<p>The operative denial rested on three substantive grounds, none involving AI detection. First, Judge Pittman rejected the defense's reading of the section 3143(a)(1) exception as "an abysmal reading of the statute." Second, he held that the defendant had not shown his guideline range would start at zero months: although the range "has yet to be finally calculated," the court said it was within its discretion to set it at 15 to 21 months once relevant conduct, including perjury, was counted. Third, he called Bhengura "a paradigmatic flight-risk" — "a convicted felon and foreign national with substantial financial assets and ample foreign contacts" — noting that the Department of Homeland Security had recently issued an immigration detainer (ECF 82).</p>

<p>On November 10, Bhengura filed a notice of interlocutory appeal of ECF 83 to the Fifth Circuit (ECF 84) and, separately, a motion asking the court to reconsider its October 27 order warning Herrera (ECF 85). The appeal's disposition is not yet known.</p>

</section>

<section class="section" id="signal">
<h2><span class="num">05 — Signal</span>The Number in the Record</h2>

<p>The percentage entered the record of a federal criminal liberty dispute through a party brief. It was not produced under a judicial directive. No defense response to the footnote appears on the docket before the ruling two days later. No validation of the tool on legal briefs appears in the record. The tool's own maker says its number is "a share of the text, not a confidence level," that "co-authorship is a spectrum," and that any detection result warrants investigation before punitive action. The footnote text gives only aggregate percentages, not the per-segment confidence ratings Pangram's own guidance says to check before acting. The scan reports themselves (Exhibits A-H) are not publicly available.</p>

<p>The court cited "ECF No. 81 at 4," the page containing the Pangram footnote, in a sanctions warning. But the judge's criticisms of the defense briefs are substantive and independent of any detector output. He identified a misapplied case. He rejected a statutory reading on its merits. He calculated a guideline range. He assessed flight risk. Nothing in the opinion's reasoning about the briefs depends on a percentage.</p>

<p>The government would characterize the percentages differently — not as rhetorical strategy but as factual disclosure of the extent of AI use. This characterization has force. "Portions of this brief" does not tell a court how much of a filing was machine-generated, and a prosecutor has an interest in making that quantity visible. But the structural observation remains — and it is interpretation, labeled as such. When a court rule provides both permission and a judicial mechanism for further specificity, and a party deploys a commercial tool outside that mechanism, the resulting number enters the record through a pathway that is less procedurally controlled but looks more precise.</p>

<p>The rule said disclose. Counsel disclosed. A percentage followed anyway — through a pathway the rule had not directed and the court had not invoked. The number was not the basis for the ruling. The number is in the record. Wherever permissive AI rules meet inexpensive detection tools, the same dynamic can recur: disclosure invites detection, detection produces a figure, and the figure enters the proceeding through a channel the rule's own specificity mechanism was designed to control. If the rational incentive for an attorney considering AI disclosure is shaped by the knowledge that candor triggers quantification, the architects of these rules face a design question they did not anticipate.</p>

</section>
